# The Daedric Princes

In Devotion, your character builds a religious life through the deeds they do, the god they honor, and the world they move through. Most of that life points toward the safe, ordinary gods of your people. This guide is about the other road: the sixteen Daedric Princes, the powerful and dangerous spirits you can deal with no matter what race you are.

If you have not read the "How Devotion Works" primer yet, read it first. This guide assumes you already understand piety, the tiers, and how devotion is tracked. Everything here builds on that.

[size=5][b]Walking the Daedric Road[/b][/size]

Every race can deal with a Daedric Prince. None of them can do it safely.

Honoring a Prince is never the civic, respectable path. It is not like lighting a candle at a temple of Mara or making an honest day's offering to Zenithar. A Prince's regard is a bargain. You give something to get something, and the something you give is rarely small.

That bargain shows up the same way your normal devotion does. The Prince notices what you do. You climb from being barely noticed, to being noticed, to being claimed. The piety bar still fills the same way, separately for each Prince, capped at about the same gain each day. But the meaning underneath is darker. Where a god might reward kindness, a Prince may reward cruelty, theft, ruin, or madness. The road runs the same direction. It just runs through worse country.

A few things stay true no matter which Prince you walk toward:

[list]
[*]The deed has to fit. A Prince only cares about acts inside their own sphere. Killing the helpless means nothing to Meridia but pleases Molag Bal. Reading forbidden books means nothing to Malacath but feeds Hermaeus Mora.
[*]Repeating the exact same deed earns less each time, just like ordinary devotion. Variety matters far more than grinding.
[*]You can usually walk back out. But walking out leaves a mark.
[/list]

[size=5][b]The Sixteen Princes[/b][/size]

These are the sixteen Princes you can reach in Skyrim. Each rewards a different kind of life. Here is what each one wants from you, in a single line.

[list]
[*][b]Azura[/b] - twilight, fate, and prophecy. She favors mercy shown to the cursed and outcast, foresight, and the dawn-and-dusk watch. Her Star is her gift.
[*][b]Boethiah[/b] - struggle and the overthrow of false authority. She rewards proving yourself through open battle, hard self-betterment, betrayal as a test, and the culling of the unworthy.
[*][b]Mephala[/b] - secrets, webs, and the hidden blade. She favors stealth, manipulation, quiet murder, and crossing into forbidden places.
[*][b]Malacath[/b] - the harsh code of the spurned. He rewards strength proven in fair fight, the work of the forge, and enduring hardship. He despises striking the defenseless and the thief's sneaking.
[*][b]Meridia[/b] - the living light and the burning of the dead. She rewards destroying the undead and rejecting necromancy. Raising the dead is the deepest offense against her.
[*][b]Hircine[/b] - the great hunt. He favors the kill of worthy prey, the chase, the night under open sky, and the beast's shape. He is one of the two Princes reached mainly through a curse.
[*][b]Molag Bal[/b] - domination and the breaking of the weak. He rewards cruelty to the helpless, taking by force, and the violation of soul and body. He is the other curse-linked Prince.
[*][b]Nocturnal[/b] - shadow, luck, and debt. She rewards the picked lock, the quiet theft, and the unseen way. Her gifts always come with a price that comes due.
[*][b]Hermaeus Mora[/b] - forbidden knowledge endlessly hoarded. He favors reading lore and spell tomes, discovering hidden places, and learning every secret there is.
[*][b]Mehrunes Dagon[/b] - destruction, ruin, and revolution. He rewards cutting down what stands, breaking the mighty, and terror. Building and preserving offend him.
[*][b]Sheogorath[/b] - madness and the breaking of routine. He delights in the absurd, the novel, and the unpredictable. The orderly and the dull bore him.
[*][b]Namira[/b] - revulsion, decay, and the outcast's hunger. She is reached through cannibalism, the embrace of rot, and solidarity with rejected things.
[*][b]Sanguine[/b] - excess and indulgence. He favors the feast, the drink, and the revel. Sober restraint cuts his party short.
[*][b]Clavicus Vile[/b] - the bargain and the wish. He rewards the deal struck, power claimed by terms rather than toil, and the loophole. He prefers a bargain to honest work or simple theft.
[*][b]Peryite[/b] - plague, order, and the lowest task. He favors patient humble labor kept in its proper place, and the bearing of disease. Curing affliction undoes the order he imposes.
[*][b]Vaermina[/b] - dreams, nightmares, and stolen memory. She visits the sleeper, harvests the dream, and feeds on fear and forbidden lore.
[/list]

